# Magician patch manifest
#
# Every range of the rebuilt PRG that is filled from the cartridge rather than
# from Eurocom's released source. This file contains **no cartridge bytes**: it
# is a list of claims, each with a digest to check them against, and
# `asm/patches.py` is the only thing that turns a claim into bytes. The cartridge
# is read, never written, and never committed -- see LEGAL.md.
#
# ## Fields
#
#   region <name>   start a region
#   class    a|b|c|d|e why the range is filled. See below -- this is the field
#                     that stops a rising match percentage from being
#                     indistinguishable from a rising number of hidden bugs.
#   cart     <dump>  which dump on this machine the bytes come from
#   sha1     <hex>   that dump's body SHA1 (header stripped). Checked against
#                     BOTH the manifest's own registry and the file on disk.
#   prg   <off> <len>  PRG file offset and byte count (hex; `0d` prefix for decimal)
#   cpu      <addr>  the CPU address that offset is presented at, for reading
#   reason   <text>  required. Repeated lines are appended.
#   end            finish the region
#
# ## The five classes
#
#   a  placement bug. The source is right and the module is in the wrong 8 KiB
#      slot. **Fix the placement.** Copying the cartridge over it destroys the
#      only evidence of the bug.
#   b  a hole. The source emits *nothing* at this address and the target cartridge
#      emits bytes. Measured against Beta 1 (1990-03-02), the build this source
#      came from, this class is currently **empty** -- see below.
#   c  assembler bug. The source is present but `asm/pds6502.py` mis-assembles
#      it. **Fix the assembler.**
#   d  data / CHR packing order. A separate problem from code.
#   e  occupied by the wrong asset. The source *does* emit bytes here, they are
#      wrong, and no re-placement fixes them, because what belongs here is an
#      asset this source does not contain in any form. b and e are both filled
#      the same way and are not the same finding: `b` says the source stopped
#      short, `e` says the source was working from different data, which in
#      practice means the layout around it diverged too.
#
# Only `b` and `e` may be filled. Every `a` and `c` region found so far is
# recorded in GAPMAP.md with its evidence and is deliberately *not* filled.
#
# ## The manifest is empty, and that is a finding
#
# It had one region, `sam-samples-at-fc40`, class `b`, filled from the release.
# It is gone because the region was an artifact of targeting the release.
#
# `X7.PDS:997` says `load mus\sam.sam,$fb80`, and the assembler puts it at $FB80
# exactly as written. The region recorded that the release has the samples at
# $FC40 instead, moved up $C0 bytes, and that this was therefore a hole.
#
# Against Beta 1 there is no hole. Measured over SAM.SAM's full 1136 bytes at the
# address the source itself states:
#
#   $FB80-$FFF0, source-only build vs beta1   1136/1136 bytes identical
#   $FB80-$FFF0, source-only build vs release     24/1136 bytes identical
#
# All six dumps place SAM.SAM at one of exactly two addresses:
#
#   $FB80   beta1, and this build
#   $FC40   beta2, beta3, beta4, release
#
# So the source states the Beta 1 address correctly, and the region was filling
# 864 bytes that were *already right* with the release's layout. Against Beta 1
# that is a net **-850** bytes: it turned a correct $FB80 into the release's
# different content at $FC40. This is the exact failure the class field exists to
# prevent -- the region's own report said "+850 matched", because it measured
# itself against the release it named, while the headline measured Beta 1.
#
# The general lesson, which is why it is written down here rather than in a
# journal entry: **a region must be re-derived whenever the target changes.** A
# manifest region is a claim about one specific dump, and the digest check only
# proves the bytes came from the dump the region named -- it cannot tell you the
# region is still the right claim.
#
# ## Class `e`: the title scene descriptors, re-derived
#
# Class `e` was added for the title and password scene descriptors. Against the
# release both were unresolvable, and the reason given was a 993-byte layout
# divergence: this build put `pan.dat` at slot-7 offset $0E05 and the release at
# $11E6, and the release's bytes at this build's `tit.dat` and `pw.dat` offsets
# were 6502 code rather than descriptors.
#
# Against Beta 1 that gap is 120 bytes, not 993 -- `pan.dat` is at slot-7 offset
# $0D8D, this build puts it at $0E05 -- and Beta 1 has a real `TIT.DAT` at PRG
# 60255 where the release has none. So the descriptors are present after all and
# the class-`e` "absent work" was the release rewriting the title screen. The
# remaining 120 bytes is a real layout question and is not papered over; see
# GAPMAP.md.

version 1
